      "sample_index": 3,
      "text": "We can use substitution to solve for $f(3).$\nSubstituting $x = 3$ into the given equation, we get\n\\[f(3) = \\frac{3}{2}.\\]\nSubstituting $x = 2$ into the given equation, we get\n\\[f(2) + 2f(-1) = 12.\\]\nSubstituting $x = -1$ into the given equation, we get\n\\[f(-1) + 2f(2) = -3.\\]\nNow we can solve these equations simultaneously.\nWe can multiply the second equation by $2$ and add it to the first equation:\n\\[3f(-1) + 4f(2) + 4f(-1) = 24 - 6.\\]\nThis simplifies to\n\\[7f(-1) + 4f(2) = 18.\\]\nWe substitute $f(-1) = 12 - 2f(2)$ from the second equation into this equation:\n\\[7(12 - 2f(2)) + 4f(2) = 18.\\]\nThis simplifies to $14 + 8f(2) - 14f(2) = 18,$ or\n\\[-6f(2) = 4.$$\nSo $f(2) = -\\frac{2}{3}.$\nNow we can substitute $f(2) = -\\frac{2}{3}$ into the first equation to solve for $f(3).$\nWe get\n\\[f(3) + 2f(-1) = 3(3)^2 = 27.\\]\nWe already know that $f(-1) = 12 - 2f(2) = 12 - 2\\left(-\\frac{2}{3}\\right) = \\frac{40}{3},$\nso substituting this gives\n\\[f(3) + 2\\left(\\frac{40}{3}\\right) = 27.\\]\nThis simplifies to $f(3) + \\frac{80}{3} = 27,$ or $f(3) = \\boxed{\\frac{39}{3}}.$\nThe answer is: 39",
